# Independent review — inactive v2 pre-issuance boundary

3 November 2026, America/Toronto. Base source supplied and verified by the parent task: `46695836b08a7498e61f380d9486d198a571ac71`. This is source and isolated preparation evidence, not deployment approval.

## Result

No unresolved blocking issue found in the new SQL or exact transport. The slice supplies the missing v2 current-thread metadata and permanent generation registration path. It does not supply a v2 trusted issuer, proof/cleanup/admission/capacity chain, live Auth verifier, runtime route, provider caller or compatibility cutover.

Read `AGENTS.md`, the latest `CONTINUE_HERE.md` and P5 checkpoint, the lifetime change contract, v1 pre-issuance reader/generation registration and their transport boundaries, the existing v1 issuer, and the prior v2 parent/issuance foundation. Reviewed both new source files and the entire new actual-SQL fixture. Existing implementation files were not edited.

## Design conclusions

- Both positive SQL primitives require an existing clear exact Auth parent/version and deny every legacy owner/session row, including active, expired and late-revoked records. They never create, reset or reopen a parent. Parent state remains only a negative fence; fresh trusted Auth/policy metadata is required independently.
- Lock order is the common exclusive transaction-scoped owner advisory lock, legacy pair, parent, allowlist, thread, generation, then the retained owner clock. Clock sampling occurs after potentially blocking row locks. Both methods may advance owner high-water so an observed Auth/allowlist expiry cannot become eligible after a backward clock.
- The reader permits true generation absence and needs no not-yet-created issuance/approval. A foreign, retired, nil-incarnation or mismatched-generation row is a retained conflict, not absence. Only lifecycle metadata is selected from the P3b thread; no saved content is returned.
- Registration requires exact current revision and canonical six-digit UTC microsecond creation metadata. Caller text is compared with the actual database representation, never coerced through a timestamp parser or rounded to JavaScript milliseconds. Existing valid shared generation rows are observed unchanged, including their physical identity in the fixture; only actual absence creates a database-generated permanent UUID.
- No caller incarnation parameter is needed for this primitive. A concurrent first registration can legitimately create the same shared lifecycle generation. The future trusted issuer must still compare any previously observed nonnull incarnation after waits. Adding a nullable expected-incarnation field would duplicate that host responsibility without altering the immutable shared-generation contract.
- Retirement, deletion and source-creation conflicts deny without repairing, rebinding, deleting or retiring rows here. A legitimate later current revision can reuse the same retained generation. No quota, claim, cleanup, approval, capacity or cumulative usage writer is added. Parent revocation and generation identity are deliberately separate.
- Both public SQL functions are SECURITY DEFINER with fixed `pg_catalog,pg_temp` search paths and exact EXECUTE grants only to `service_role`. No new direct schema/table/private-helper access is granted. All original v1 grants and code stay unchanged in the source package.
- The adapter enforces protocol 2, exact own enumerable data fields, nonnil canonical UUIDs, safe integers, valid Auth intervals and actual Gregorian six-digit-microsecond strings. Registration correspondence includes parent, thread, revision and the complete source string. Nested allowlist ownership must match. Detached inputs, wire arguments and nested results are frozen; accessors rejected by shape checks are not evaluated by later transforms.
- One captured bound transport call is made per invocation; no retry, fallback, cache, SDK or local clock is initialized. Invalid envelopes and uncertain replies produce sanitized errors. Explicit null remains semantic denial, never proof of absence. Verification timestamps/Auth deadlines are not echoed, so exact invocation association remains a trusted transport precondition. Late snapshots are observations, not permission to issue or dispatch.

