## Actual SQL fixture review and limits

The new fixture uses real protected-role transactions, checks 22 tables, preserves deliberately seeded nonzero accounting and unrelated owners, restores controlled clock changes and closes both database instances. It revokes service execution of the old generation registrar in the isolated fixture before exercising v2 read → generation registration → bound issuance. This directly tests removal of the previous legacy-generation dependency, not the complete planned cutover.

Other reviewed cases cover no-parent/revoke-before-use denial, all legacy fence states, late revocation, saturated parent version, exact microseconds and database display settings, actual P3b Stop/Delete, retained conflict/FK behavior, verification and expiry boundaries, high-water rollback, unsafe values/current policy, committed lost/malformed/late replies and a trigger-induced failed insertion that rolls back both generation and clock. Snapshot comparisons distinguish permitted clock/new-generation mutations from unrelated data and cumulative holds. Fixture-authenticated metadata and transport losses are explicitly fictional; one connection is not native concurrency, HTTP fault injection or provider/device evidence. Parent and fixture agent own execution counts and final result.

